tools.description:
Search RedBison's 600+ tools by keyword: developer utilities, file and media converters, and finance, health, math and engineering calculators. Returns a ranked list with each tool's canonical URL and, when one exists, its in-depth guide. Use this when a user asks 'is there a tool for X?' or 'what RedBison tool should I use to Y?'

Returns: JSON with { query, count, results[] } where each result has { id, name, description, url, category }.
Example input: {"query":"json format","limit":5}

Get full metadata for a specific RedBison tool by its id (slug).

Returns: Full JSON metadata for the tool: { id, name, description, url, category, subcategory?, keywords[] }.
Example input: {"id":"jwt-decoder"}

List all tool categories and subcategories available on RedBison.

Returns: JSON array of { category, count } entries covering the full RedBison catalog.
Example input: {}

Return server status, name, version, and uptime info. Use this to monitor the MCP server.

Returns: JSON { status, name, version, startedAt, timestamp }.
Example input: {}

Base64-encode a UTF-8 string.

Returns: Base64-encoded string (UTF-8 safe).
Example input: {"text":"hello world"}

Base64-decode a string back to UTF-8.

Returns: UTF-8 decoded plain text.
Example input: {"text":"aGVsbG8gd29ybGQ="}

Percent-encode a string for use in URLs.

Returns: Percent-encoded string suitable for URL query values.
Example input: {"text":"hello world & friends"}

Percent-decode a URL-encoded string.

Returns: Percent-decoded plain text.
Example input: {"text":"hello%20world%20%26%20friends"}

Pretty-print an HTML fragment.

Returns: Pretty-printed HTML string with normalized indentation.
Example input: {"html":"<div><p>hi</p><p>there</p></div>","indent":2}

Pretty-print an XML document.

Returns: Pretty-printed XML string with normalized indentation.
Example input: {"xml":"<root><a>1</a><b>2</b></root>","indent":2}

Format a SQL query with keyword uppercasing and line breaks before major clauses.

Returns: Formatted SQL query with keywords on new lines.
Example input: {"sql":"select id,name from users where active=true order by name"}

Convert text to a URL-safe slug.

Returns: URL-safe slug (lowercase, dashes).
Example input: {"text":"Hello, World! 2026"}

Remove duplicate lines from text while preserving first-occurrence order.

Returns: Text with duplicate lines removed (order preserved).
Example input: {"text":"apple\nbanana\napple\ncherry","caseInsensitive":false,"trim":true}

Collapse runs of whitespace inside each line to a single space and trim ends.

Returns: Text with runs of whitespace collapsed to a single space.
Example input: {"text":"hello    world   foo"}

Join lines with a separator (comma by default). Empty lines are skipped.

Returns: Single-line string with items joined by the chosen separator.
Example input: {"text":"apple\nbanana\ncherry","separator":", ","quote":false}

Split a delimited string into one item per line.

Returns: Multi-line string, one item per line.
Example input: {"text":"apple, banana, cherry","separator":",","trim":true}

Generate random human names.

Returns: JSON array of random human name strings.
Example input: {"count":3}

Generate random-looking (non-deliverable) test email addresses.

Returns: JSON array of random email address strings.
Example input: {"count":3,"domain":"example.com"}

Generate cryptographically random numbers in a range.

Returns: JSON array of generated numbers.
Example input: {"min":1,"max":100,"count":5,"mode":"integer"}

Generate placeholder lorem ipsum text.

Returns: Lorem ipsum text of the requested size.
Example input: {"unit":"paragraphs","count":2}

Compute the equated monthly installment (EMI) for a loan.

Returns: JSON { emi, totalInterest, totalPayment } for the loan.
Example input: {"principal":500000,"annualRatePercent":8.5,"months":240}

Compute simple interest: I = P × r × t.

Returns: JSON { interest, total } computed as P·r·t.
Example input: {"principal":10000,"annualRatePercent":5,"years":3}

Compute compound interest: A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt).

Returns: JSON { interest, total } computed as P(1+r/n)^(nt).
Example input: {"principal":10000,"annualRatePercent":5,"years":10,"compoundsPerYear":12}

Calculate Body Mass Index in metric (kg/cm) or imperial (lb/in) units.

Returns: JSON { bmi, category } (Underweight / Normal / Overweight / Obese).
Example input: {"weight":70,"height":175,"units":"metric"}

List the calculators that run_calculator can execute (finance, health, maths, statistics, construction, science, everyday and engineering), with each one's inputs, units, defaults and an example. Filter with an optional search query.

Returns: JSON { total, calculators[] } where each has { id, title, summary, url, inputs (JSON Schema per field), example }.
Example input: {"query":"mortgage","limit":5}

Run any RedBison calculator by id with named inputs and get the result as JSON: mortgages and loans, savings and investing, pay and tax, body and fitness, maths, statistics, geometry, construction, science and engineering (beams, bolts, wire size, PCB traces, pipe flow, rafters, sheet metal and more). Call list_calculators first to see ids, inputs and units.

Returns: JSON { id, title, url, inputs, result } with the calculator's outputs.
Example input: {"id":"percentage-calculator","inputs":{}}

Render text as a FIGlet ASCII art banner in one of 7 bundled fonts (Standard, Slant, Small, Big, Doom, ANSI Shadow, Mini), optionally wrapped at a column width and inside code comment markers. Printable ASCII only. The page at redbison.dev/ascii-art-text-generator has more fonts.

Returns: Text: the banner (show it in a monospace code block). structuredContent { art, font, lines, columns, unsupported[] }.
Example input: {"text":"Hello","font":"Slant","layout":"default","width":80,"comment":"none"}

Compute or validate the GS1 mod-10 check digit of an EAN-8, UPC-A, EAN-13 or ITF-14 (GTIN-8/12/13/14) number. Give the digits without the check digit to compute it, or the full code to validate it.

Returns: JSON { digits, checkDigit, fullCode, valid, gtin14, formats[] }. valid is true/false when a full code was checked and null when the check digit was computed.
Example input: {"code":"400638133393","mode":"compute"}

Combine .gitignore templates (based on github/gitignore) for languages, frameworks, tools, editors and operating systems into one deduplicated file, optionally test paths against it. Template ids: node, python, django, jupyter, java, maven, gradle, kotlin, android, go, rust, ruby, rails, php, laravel, dotnet, c, cpp, swift, flutter, elixir, scala, r, nextjs, terraform, unity, docker, secrets, vscode, jetbrains, visualstudio, eclipse, vim, emacs, sublime, macos, windows, linux.

Returns: JSON { content (.gitignore text), unknownTemplates[], duplicatesRemoved, tests[{ path, ignored, rule{pattern,line,negate}, via }] }.
Example input: {"templates":["node","macos","vscode"],"testPaths":[".env","node_modules/x.js",".vscode/settings.json"]}

Look up HTTP status codes: meaning, defining RFC section (RFC 9110 and others from the IANA registry), when to send it and common pitfalls. Pass a code (404), a class (5xx) or words (rate limit). Unofficial nginx and Cloudflare codes are included and flagged.

Returns: JSON { query, count, results[] } where each result has { code, name, class, spec, official, deprecated, cacheableByDefault, summary, usage, note }, plus unknownCode text when a 3-digit code is not registered.
Example input: {"query":"429"}

Find the MIME (media) type for a file extension or file name, or the extensions for a MIME type. Returns the type, whether it is IANA-registered, aliases, notes and the Content-Type header to send.

Returns: JSON { query, matchedBy: 'extension'|'mime'|'text', count, results[] } with { mime, extensions[], name, category, registered, aliases[], spec, note, contentType }.
Example input: {"query":"woff2"}

Translate text to International Morse code (ITU-R M.1677-1) or Morse back to text. Letters are separated by spaces and words by ' / '. Wrap prosigns in angle brackets (<SOS>, <AR>, <SK>). Also returns the transmission time at a given speed in words per minute, with optional Farnsworth spacing. Same rules as redbison.dev/morse-code-translator.

Returns: Text: the Morse or decoded text. structuredContent { direction, morse | text, unsupported[] | unknownCodes[], durationSeconds, unitMs }.
Example input: {"input":"SOS at 18 wpm","direction":"encode","wpm":18}

Spell text with the ICAO/NATO radiotelephony alphabet (Alfa, Bravo, Charlie...), with ICAO number words (Tree, Fower, Fife, Niner) or plain ones, optional Capital/Lowercase markers for passwords, or decode code words back to text. Same rules as redbison.dev/nato-phonetic-alphabet.

Returns: Text: code words, ' / ' between input words. structuredContent { spelled, tokens[{char, word, pronunciation}] } or, for decode, { text, unknown[] }.
Example input: {"text":"Gate B12","direction":"spell","spelling":"icao","digits":"icao","markCase":false}

Score English text with Flesch Reading Ease, Flesch-Kincaid Grade, Gunning Fog, SMOG, Coleman-Liau and the Automated Readability Index, and list hard sentences, passive voice, -ly adverbs and wordy phrases with plainer alternatives. Syllables are estimated with rules plus an exception list. Same rules as redbison.dev/readability-checker.

Returns: JSON { counts, wordsPerSentence, syllablesPerWord, fleschReadingEase, fleschBand, fleschKincaidGrade, gunningFog, smog, smogShortSample, colemanLiau, automatedReadabilityIndex, averageGrade, hardSentences, veryHardSentences, passiveVoice[], adverbs[], plainerWords[{found,try}], hardest[{sentence,words,grade,level}] }.
Example input: {"text":"The committee utilized a methodology that was developed in order to facilitate a comprehensive assessment of the program. We read it twice.","lineBreaksEndSentences":true,"maxSentences":5}

Generate 301/302/307/308 redirect rules for Apache .htaccess (mod_rewrite), nginx, Netlify _redirects or vercel.json from a list of 'source destination [code]' lines. A source ending in /* moves a whole folder. Also checks for loops, chains and duplicates and can add HTTPS and www/non-www rules.

Returns: JSON { platform, config (file text), ruleCount, issues[{ line, level, message }], notes[] }.
Example input: {"rules":"/old-page /new-page\n/blog/* /news/:splat 301","platform":"nginx"}

Parse a User-Agent header into browser, engine, OS, device type, vendor, model and CPU (ua-parser-js), and detect search, AI, social, SEO and monitoring bots and HTTP libraries. Notes explain User-Agent reduction limits such as Windows 11 reporting as Windows NT 10.0.

Returns: JSON { summary, browser{name,version,major}, engine{name,version}, os{name,version}, device{type,vendor,model}, cpu{architecture}, isBot, bot{name,category,owner}|null, notes[] }.
Example input: {"userAgent":"Mozilla/5.0 (iPhone; CPU iPhone OS 17_5 like Mac OS X) AppleWebKit/605.1.15 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/17.5 Mobile/15E148 Safari/604.1"}

Add Google Analytics UTM parameters (utm_source, utm_medium, utm_campaign, utm_id, utm_term, utm_content) to a landing page URL. Keeps existing query parameters and the #fragment, lowercases values by default, and predicts the GA4 default channel group.

Returns: JSON { url, params, predictedChannel, warnings[] }: the tagged URL, the UTM values used, the likely GA4 default channel group (approximation) and any naming warnings.
Example input: {"url":"https://example.com/pricing?plan=pro","source":"newsletter","medium":"email","campaign":"October Launch","content":"hero_button"}

Count words, characters (with and without spaces), sentences, paragraphs and lines in text, estimate reading and speaking time, list the most frequent words and check the text against common platform limits (X post weighted length, Bluesky, LinkedIn, SMS segments, meta description). Same rules as redbison.dev/word-counter.

Returns: JSON { words, characters, charactersNoSpaces, sentences, paragraphs, lines, uniqueWords, utf8Bytes, averageWordLength, averageSentenceLength, readingTime, readingSeconds, speakingTime, speakingSeconds, topWords[{word,count,percent}], platformLimits[{platform,limit,used,remaining,over,detail}] }.
Example input: {"text":"Short sentences help. Long ones, full of clauses that wander, do not.","readingWpm":238,"speakingWpm":150,"topWords":5}

Converts between 24-hour military time (0930, 17:45) and 12-hour AM/PM time, and gives the spoken military reading such as 'zero nine thirty'. The page at redbison.dev/military-time-converter shows both directions side by side.

Returns: Text: '1430 = 2:30 PM ("fourteen thirty")'. structuredContent { military, civilian, spoken }.
Example input: {"time":"1430","direction":"toCivilian"}

Writes a number out in US English words (short scale, up to 999 trillion): cardinal words, the ordinal, and the amount line used on a check. Decimals are read digit by digit. The page at redbison.dev/number-to-words-converter adds copy buttons.

Returns: Text: the number in words. structuredContent { words, wholePart, ordinal, check }.
Example input: {"value":"1234.56"}

Encodes text to UTF-8 binary or hex bytes, or decodes binary/hex back to text with strict UTF-8 validation (bad byte sequences are rejected, not replaced). The page at redbison.dev/binary-text-converter shows both directions live.

Returns: Text: the encoded bytes or decoded text. structuredContent { output, mode }.
Example input: {"input":"Hi","mode":"textToBinary"}

Parses any CSS color (hex #abc/#aabbcc/#aabbccdd, rgb()/rgba(), hsl()/hsla(), optional alpha) and returns it in hex, rgb() and hsl() forms. The page at redbison.dev/hex-to-rgb-converter adds a live swatch.

Returns: Text: the color in all three forms. structuredContent { hex, rgb, hsl }.
Example input: {"color":"#ff3131"}

Gives the exact dimensions of ISO 216 (A and B series) and North American paper sizes in millimeters and inches, plus the closest size in the other system (A4 pairs with Letter). The page at redbison.dev/paper-size-converter shows the full chart.

Returns: Text: dimensions and the cross-system match. structuredContent { name, mm, inches, system, closestOtherSystem }.
Example input: {"size":"A4"}

Converts ring sizes between US, UK letters, EU/ISO circumference and Japanese scales, from a US size (3-13 in halves) or a measured inside diameter in mm. UK letter charts vary by up to half a size between jewelers. The page at redbison.dev/ring-size-converter shows the full chart.

Returns: Text: the size in all four systems. structuredContent { us, uk, euIso, japan, diameterMm, circumferenceMm }.
Example input: {"us":7}

Explain how to use any RedBison MCP tool, with its input schema, a sample input and the sample output it produces. Call with no arguments for the list of tools; pass `tool` for one tool, and `calculator` with tool 'run_calculator' for one calculator.

Returns: JSON with a tool's { usage, returns, inputSchema, sampleInput, sampleOutput }, or the list of tools when called with no arguments.
Example input: {"tool":"list_calculators"}
